VLAN Troubleshooting (Day 5)

These are the two switch related faults I broke on purpose in my Packet Tracer lab and then fixed. I did one at a time and made sure everything worked again before starting the next one.

Lab setup: SW1-CORE and SW2-ACCESS are connected by a trunk on Gi0/1. R1-CORE does router on a stick off SW1. There are six VLANs: 10 IT, 20 HR, 30 Finance, 40 Servers, 50 Security and 60 Guest.


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 Fault 1 - PC-IT in the wrong VLAN

Symptom:

PC-IT (192.168.10.10) could not reach its gateway. When I pinged it from the router it failed 0/5. Pings from the router to the other subnets (192.168.20.10 and 192.168.60.10) still worked, so it was only this one host.

What I did to break it:

    SW1-CORE(config)# interface fa0/2
    SW1-CORE(config-if)# switchport access vlan 20
    SW1-CORE(config-if)# exit

Commands used to diagnose:

    SW1-CORE# show vlan brief
    Router> ping 192.168.10.10

show vlan brief had Fa0/2 listed under VLAN 20 (HR), and VLAN 10 had no active ports at all.

Root cause:

Fa0/2 is the port PC-IT is plugged into and it was assigned to VLAN 20 instead of VLAN 10. So the PC was sitting in the HR broadcast domain with an IT address and the router's VLAN 10 sub-interface could not see it.

Fix:

    SW1-CORE> enable
    SW1-CORE# configure terminal
    SW1-CORE(config)# interface fastethernet 0/2
    SW1-CORE(config-if)# switchport access vlan 10
    SW1-CORE(config-if)# exit
    SW1-CORE(config)# exit
    SW1-CORE# write memory

Verification:

I pinged PC-IT from the router again and it was 100 percent.

    Router> ping 192.168.10.10

    Type escape sequence to abort.
    Sending 5, 100-byte ICMP Echos to 192.168.10.10, timeout is 2 seconds:
    !!!!!
    Success rate is 100 percent (5/5), round-trip min/avg/max = 0/1/4 ms


